Item 08 is done. Under the repaired rules, the pathwidth benchmarks prove no width differently from the old results.

- **Widths:** 11,424 graphs were proved by both the old run and the new one, and all have the same width. No width proved on one side contradicts an upper bound from the other. All 50 VSPLIB trees come out at the width their name encodes, grids with sides 5–13 at their side, and all 14 of Coudert et al.'s Table 4 graphs that we hold at their published values.
- **Graphs proved on one side only:** 13 Rome graphs were proved by the old run and timed out at 600 s in the new one. Rerun with a 3,600 s cap, all 13 prove the old width, in 386–565 s, so the miss was the clock under heavier load. So every width the old results proved is reproduced under the repaired rules. Another 24 Rome graphs are proved for the first time, which brings Rome to 11,194 of 11,534 (97.05%, against 97.0% before).
- **Cost in nodes:** on graphs both runs proved by refutation, the repaired rules use 0.3% to 1.7% more nodes per set. The median pair is unchanged. The worst single graph is `grafo6585.97` at 1.64×. Node counts are deterministic: rerunning with the old rules today gives exactly the old counts, so these differences come from the rules, not from noise.
- **The two result-file issues in `TRANSFER.md` are fixed** in `bench/run.py`:
  - the `name` column is now the instance's path, so the 50 tree rows get 50 distinct keys instead of 35;
  - `proof` is empty when a width is unproved, and errors go to their own column.
- **Other changes to `bench/run.py`:**
  - a column recording which rules ran;
  - resume, so a stopped run continues where its file ends;
  - a hard per-graph kill: `DorogovtsevGoltsevMendesGraph` had ignored its time budget for 4,007 s in the first sweep.

  `bench/summary.py` reads both the old and new formats. The old result files are unchanged, since they are the baseline. There are 4 new tests in `pathwidth_solver/tests/test_bench_run.py`.

The gate passes (1,356 MOSP tests, 114 pathwidth_solver tests). The rerun took about 131 core-hours, and nothing was written to `solutions/`.

**Caveat:** some tree file names repeat across the three rotation folders, and the old results can't say which file a row was. Those trees are compared on width only, so 26 of the 50 trees are in the node comparison.

